WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.451
Algorithm analysis begins by separating three
layers.

2
00:00:04.451 --> 00:00:09.081
The problem states the required relationship
between inputs and outputs.

3
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:13.711
An algorithm is a language-independent
procedure for producing the output.

4
00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:19.323
An implementation expresses that procedure in
a programming language and on a machine.

5
00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:26.020
Two programs may implement the same algorithm,
and two algorithms may solve the same problem

6
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.806
with very different costs.

7
00:00:27.806 --> 00:00:33.828
State assumptions about input size, allowed
operations, and required correctness before

8
00:00:33.828 --> 00:00:34.923
comparing performance.

9
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:40.361
Otherwise a faster-looking program may be
solving an easier problem or relying on an

10
00:00:40.361 --> 00:00:42.691
operation whose cost has been ignored.

11
00:00:42.691 --> 00:00:47.576
Analysis makes comparisons meaningful by
fixing the question first.

12
00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:51.160
Performance cannot rescue an incorrect result.

13
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.842
A correctness argument begins with
preconditions describing allowed inputs and a

14
00:00:56.842 --> 00:00:59.425
postcondition describing the required output.

15
00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:05.156
For iterative algorithms, a loop invariant
connects the work already completed to the

16
00:01:05.156 --> 00:01:06.479
work that remains.

17
00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:12.899
Show that the invariant is true initially,
preserved by each iteration, and strong enough

18
00:01:12.899 --> 00:01:15.650
to imply the postcondition at termination.

19
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:22.248
For recursive algorithms, prove the base case
and show that correct solutions to smaller

20
00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.076
subproblems produce a correct larger solution.

21
00:01:25.076 --> 00:01:31.313
Test examples help find counterexamples, but a
proof explains why every allowed case works.

22
00:01:31.313 --> 00:01:36.262
Correctness and performance are separate
claims, and both need evidence.

23
00:01:36.386 --> 00:01:39.167
To analyze cost, define n.

24
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:45.548
It may be the number of array elements, graph
vertices and edges, digits in an integer, or

25
00:01:45.548 --> 00:01:47.050
dimensions of a matrix.

26
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:52.037
Then define which operations count as constant
time in the chosen model.

27
00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:57.705
Array access and machine-word arithmetic may
be constant under a random-access model, but

28
00:01:57.705 --> 00:02:01.629
arithmetic on integers with thousands of
digits is not.

29
00:02:01.629 --> 00:02:06.795
Hash-table lookup is commonly expected
constant time under assumptions, not

30
00:02:06.795 --> 00:02:09.894
guaranteed constant time for every input.

31
00:02:09.894 --> 00:02:11.489
State these choices.

32
00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:16.297
An asymptotic result without an input measure
or model is incomplete.

33
00:02:16.297 --> 00:02:22.552
The model is an abstraction, but a useful
abstraction exposes dominant structure while

34
00:02:22.552 --> 00:02:24.958
remaining connected to implementation reality.

35
00:02:25.106 --> 00:02:30.298
Asymptotic notation describes growth beyond a
sufficiently large input.

36
00:02:30.298 --> 00:02:36.904
Big O is an upper bound: after some threshold,
the cost is no more than a constant multiple

37
00:02:36.904 --> 00:02:38.372
of the comparison function.

38
00:02:38.372 --> 00:02:40.247
Omega is a lower bound.

39
00:02:40.247 --> 00:02:44.175
Theta states both and therefore gives a tight
growth class.

40
00:02:44.175 --> 00:02:50.594
Big O is not automatically worst case; worst,
average, and best case describe which inputs

41
00:02:50.594 --> 00:02:53.589
are considered, while O describes a bound.

42
00:02:53.589 --> 00:03:00.802
A function in Theta of n is also in O of n
squared, but the looser statement hides

43
00:03:00.802 --> 00:03:01.203
information.

44
00:03:01.203 --> 00:03:06.854
Use the tightest justified class and state
which case is being analyzed.

45
00:03:06.854 --> 00:03:13.036
Constants and lower-order terms disappear
asymptotically, not because they never matter,

46
00:03:13.036 --> 00:03:15.846
but because growth eventually dominates.

47
00:03:15.986 --> 00:03:22.571
Constant, logarithmic, linear, n log n,
quadratic, polynomial, and exponential growth

48
00:03:22.571 --> 00:03:24.966
describe very different scaling.

49
00:03:24.966 --> 00:03:31.656
Doubling n barely changes a logarithmic cost,
doubles a linear cost, roughly doubles plus an

50
00:03:31.656 --> 00:03:37.901
additional factor for n log n, quadruples a
quadratic cost, and can multiply an

51
00:03:37.901 --> 00:03:39.239
exponential cost enormously.

52
00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:43.780
This ordering guides design choices before
low-level optimization.

53
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:51.127
A carefully optimized quadratic algorithm can
beat a linear one on tiny inputs, but as n

54
00:03:51.127 --> 00:03:53.882
grows the growth-rate advantage eventually
dominates.

55
00:03:53.882 --> 00:03:58.078
Use realistic input ranges and memory
constraints too.

56
00:03:58.078 --> 00:04:03.427
Asymptotics predict scaling; they do not
replace measurement or permit ignoring large

57
00:04:03.427 --> 00:04:06.548
constants when the deployment range is known.

58
00:04:06.674 --> 00:04:11.203
For a loop, count how many times the body runs
and the cost of each run.

59
00:04:11.203 --> 00:04:16.228
A loop from zero to n minus one with constant
body work is linear.

60
00:04:16.228 --> 00:04:22.095
Two independent loops in sequence add, so
linear plus linear remains linear.

61
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:27.478
Perfectly nested loops of n iterations
multiply to n squared.

62
00:04:27.478 --> 00:04:34.131
But bounds may depend on the outer index,
producing a sum such as one plus two through

63
00:04:34.131 --> 00:04:36.088
n, still Theta n squared.

64
00:04:36.088 --> 00:04:40.412
A loop that repeatedly halves the remaining
size is logarithmic.

65
00:04:40.412 --> 00:04:44.506
Read the update and bounds rather than
counting indentation.

66
00:04:44.506 --> 00:04:48.371
If the body calls another algorithm, include
that cost.

67
00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:53.805
Writing the sum explicitly often turns
intuition into a defensible result.

68
00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.012
A recursive algorithm's cost can be modeled
with a recurrence.

69
00:04:58.012 --> 00:05:03.548
Merge sort divides an input into two halves,
recursively sorts each half, and performs

70
00:05:03.548 --> 00:05:09.874
linear merging, giving T of n equals two T of
n over two plus Theta n.

71
00:05:09.874 --> 00:05:15.715
A recursion tree shows logarithmically many
levels with linear total work per level,

72
00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:17.961
producing Theta n log n.

73
00:05:17.961 --> 00:05:24.055
Other recurrences behave differently depending
on the number and size of subproblems and the

74
00:05:24.055 --> 00:05:24.925
combination cost.

75
00:05:24.925 --> 00:05:30.030
State a base case and avoid applying a
memorized theorem without checking its

76
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:30.423
conditions.

77
00:05:30.423 --> 00:05:36.058
Recurrences are not only algebra; they mirror
the structure of the algorithm and reveal

78
00:05:36.058 --> 00:05:37.668
where the work accumulates.

79
00:05:37.802 --> 00:05:42.190
An algorithm is often a sequence of
data-structure operations.

80
00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:48.639
Searching an unsorted array is linear, while a
balanced search tree can support logarithmic

81
00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:50.481
lookup and ordered traversal.

82
00:05:50.481 --> 00:05:54.988
A hash table can provide expected
constant-time lookup under assumptions but

83
00:05:54.988 --> 00:05:57.037
does not preserve sorted order.

84
00:05:57.037 --> 00:06:02.299
A heap makes repeated minimum extraction
efficient without supporting every search

85
00:06:02.299 --> 00:06:02.777
efficiently.

86
00:06:02.777 --> 00:06:08.321
The right structure follows the operations the
problem performs most often, the guarantees it

87
00:06:08.321 --> 00:06:11.093
requires, and the memory it can afford.

88
00:06:11.093 --> 00:06:14.690
Include construction cost, not just query
cost.

89
00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:20.366
A structure that accelerates one operation may
slow updates or use more space.

90
00:06:20.366 --> 00:06:25.098
Time-space tradeoffs are algorithmic choices,
not afterthoughts.

91
00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:31.268
Benchmarks reveal constants, cache behavior,
interpreter overhead, input distributions, and

92
00:06:31.268 --> 00:06:35.480
implementation defects that an abstract model
omits.

93
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:42.414
Measure representative inputs, vary n
systematically, repeat trials, and record the

94
00:06:42.414 --> 00:06:43.044
environment.

95
00:06:43.044 --> 00:06:48.197
Avoid timing unrelated setup unless it is part
of the operation being evaluated.

96
00:06:48.197 --> 00:06:53.044
Plot time against candidate growth functions
and investigate discrepancies.

97
00:06:53.044 --> 00:06:59.732
A benchmark on five sizes cannot prove
asymptotic complexity, and an asymptotic proof

98
00:06:59.732 --> 00:07:03.847
cannot predict an exact runtime on one laptop.

99
00:07:03.847 --> 00:07:05.876
Use both forms of evidence.

100
00:07:05.876 --> 00:07:11.811
Analysis explains expected scaling under a
model; measurement checks how a particular

101
00:07:11.811 --> 00:07:14.779
implementation and workload behave in reality.

102
00:07:14.906 --> 00:07:18.287
A complete analysis answers eight questions.

103
00:07:18.287 --> 00:07:21.514
What problem and input domain are being
solved?

104
00:07:21.514 --> 00:07:23.287
Why is the algorithm correct?

105
00:07:23.287 --> 00:07:26.016
What quantity defines input size?

106
00:07:26.016 --> 00:07:28.733
Which model assigns costs to operations?

107
00:07:28.733 --> 00:07:34.958
Are we discussing worst, average, expected,
amortized, or best case?

108
00:07:34.958 --> 00:07:37.343
What tight time and space bounds follow?

109
00:07:37.343 --> 00:07:40.850
Which data-structure assumptions support those
bounds?

110
00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.861
What do measurements show over the intended
range?

111
00:07:43.861 --> 00:07:48.019
This checklist prevents Big O from becoming a
decorative label.

112
00:07:48.019 --> 00:07:53.755
The goal is an argument another person can
inspect: the algorithm returns the right

113
00:07:53.755 --> 00:07:59.081
answer, the derivation follows its structure,
the assumptions are visible, and the measured

114
00:07:59.081 --> 00:08:01.539
implementation behaves consistently with the
claim.
